---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHeader from "../../components/PageHeader.astro";
import FeedbackEngine from "../../novascope/components/FeedbackEngine.astro";

/*
 * DEFERRED, not abandoned: this page is finished and its copy is accurate, but
 * it is deliberately not carded on the /explore hub yet.
 *
 * Held back in all three places at once, which is what the other unlinked
 * pages do and what this one was missing entirely:
 *   1. `noindex` below,
 *   2. the sitemap filter in astro.config.mjs,
 *   3. EXCLUDED in scripts/search/build-index.mjs — which fails the build if
 *      this page ever stops existing, so the hold cannot rot unnoticed.
 *
 * Being unlinked but indexed was the worst of both: a reader arrived from a
 * search result having had no way to get here deliberately, with nothing
 * saying the page was being held back. Remove all three together when it gets
 * a hub card.
 */
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Feedback budget — Anna Rosen"
  description="Do massive-star winds, photoionization, and radiation pressure deliver enough momentum to expel a cluster's natal gas — and does the cluster survive if they do? An interactive budget over six simulated environments."
  path="/explore/feedback-budget"
  noindex
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Explore · interactive"
    title="The feedback budget"
    lede="Massive stars pour winds, ionizing photons, and radiation pressure into the gas they were born in. This is the accounting: how much momentum each channel delivers, whether it clears the residual gas, and whether the young cluster stays bound once the gas is gone. The cluster and gas are a real gravoturb simulation; the budget is a per-channel momentum ledger, not a hydrodynamics run."
  />

  <div class="fbwrap wrap-wide">
    <FeedbackEngine />

    <div class="fbwrap__note prose wrap">
      <p>
        Two questions, in the order the physics asks them. <strong>First:</strong> is the
        retained feedback momentum enough to lift the residual gas out of the cloud's
        potential? <strong>Then, if it is:</strong> do the stars stay bound once the gas —
        most of the mass — is gone? Across these six environments the gas always goes, but
        not at the same speed: in five it clears in less than a stellar crossing time,
        while the most massive and most tightly bound cloud takes longer than one, so its
        stars have time to adjust as the gas leaves. Every cluster stays bound either way —
        and that is not a verdict the budget decides. These clusters are born far below
        virial equilibrium, so the stars' own self-gravity holds them whatever the gas
        does. What the environment changes is the margin, and which channel does the
        clearing.
      </p>
      <p>
        The three channels also deposit momentum on different schedules. Winds and
        radiation inject at a constant rate while the stars sit on the main sequence, so
        they accumulate linearly; the H II curve is the Spitzer D-front solution
        recomputed at each step, and although the front decelerates, the shell it sweeps
        grows faster still, so photoionization accumulates faster than linearly. Winds
        enter the ledger differently again: Weaver's similarity solution sets the ceiling
        on what a hot bubble could deliver, and how much survives cooling is a calibrated
        fraction — fixed here so the momentum boost matches what 3-D simulations of
        wind-blown bubbles measure, rather than chosen to look reasonable. Two mass-loss
        recipes are offered above, each with its own calibration; which channel leads
        depends on that choice as well as on the environment.
      </p>
      <p>
        The gas balloons outward as the budget clears it, revealing the bare cluster — a
        legible stand-in for the anisotropic champagne flows and blister breakouts a full
        radiation-hydrodynamics run resolves. That physics is what my research code
        models; this piece makes its accounting visible.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .fbwrap { padding-block: var(--space-l) var(--space-3xl); }
  .fbwrap__note { margin-block-start: var(--space-2xl); }
</style>
